  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, it was sunny (smoke aloft also noted). Temperatures are \nwarming through the 80s, for the most part. Light and variable winds \nacross the forecast area, except out over north central South \nDakota, where a persistent southerly breeze 10 to 20 mph with some \nhigher gusts are happening.\n\nGSM solutions and Ensemble clusters analysis maintain some semblance \nof western CONUS ridging throughout the seven day forecast, with \nnorthwest flow aloft happening over this CWA during most of it. This \nshould support a continuation of western CONUS wildfire smoke aloft \nadvecting across this region, along with a couple of cold frontal \npassages to help keep the really warm air from building back into \nthe CWA for any sort of prolonged period of time. Model guidance \nmaintains height falls over the northern plains during the early to \nmiddle portion of next week, when one of these cold frontal passages \nis progged for Monday. Models are progging it to be too warm aloft \nSunday afternoon/evening across north central South Dakota (700hpa \ntemps +16C to +18C), despite the ~1000J/kg of CAPE and 20-30 knots \nof deep layer shear showing up along the boundary out west river \nSunday evening. When the front is working through eastern South \nDakota Monday afternoon/evening, the deep layer shear is even \nstronger (~30-45 knots), tied to ~1500-2000J/kg CAPE. And, models \nhave progged the mid-level warmth to cool down enough Monday evening \nto wear updrafts could eventually break through the weaker mid-level \ncapping inversion and lead to something potentially supercellular in \nnature (current SPC marginal/slight risk in place for this on Day \n3). The next chance at precipitation could come Thursday/night, as \nmodels prog another frontal passage at that time.\n\nOther than tomorrow (Sunday), west river, temperatures do not appear \nto get too out of hand during this 7-day forecast cycle. \nTemperatures in the 90s to around 100F degrees (10 to 15 degrees \nabove normal) are forecast throughout and west of the Missouri River \non Sunday (hottest day of the period). Prior to the frontal passage \non Monday, highs in the 80s to low 90s are expected, cwa-wide. Then \nmuch cooler conditions (near to 10 degrees below normal) are progged \nin the deterministic/ensembles solutions for Tuesday and Wednesday \nbehind the Monday frontal passage. There is supposed to be another \nfrontal passage on Thursday, but overall, it appears the airmass \nover the region will moderate a few degrees each day from Thursday \nthrough the end of the period, running generally near to as much as \n10 degrees above normal.\n\nSoutherly winds 20 to 30 mph with higher gusts are forecast over the \nregion on Sunday. Across north central South Dakota, where things \nare D2-3 drought stricken, these windy conditions will combine with \ndry fuels and RH values expected to drop below 30 percent, as well \nas temperatures running up into the 90s, to perhaps as high as 100F \ndegrees, to produce high to very high Grassland Fire Danger indices.",
